WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:05.550
Speaker 1: So we're once again in the middle
of a climate change summit, and as is often

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:12.369
the case during these climate change summits,
we are starting to see articles outraged about

00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:19.040
the fact that many world leaders and others
like Jeff Bezos flew to.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.029
In this case, it's Glasgow, Scotland, to the
Climate Change Summit on private jets.

00:00:25.029 --> 00:00:30.670
And the contrast that is illuminated by these
articles is, of course, about we're trying

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:37.450
to figure out climate change and yet private
jet on a per person basis.

00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:41.480
Outrageous carbon footprint, a private jet
where you might have two pilots and a flight

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:48.170
attendant and two people or, you know, maybe
a small entourage on a per person basis, an

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:53.840
outrageous expenditure of carbon emissions.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:59.290
And it is something that happens every time
there is one of these climate summit and it's

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:05.750
an issue and I'm going to talk about it, but
I actually believe that it's part of the distraction

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:07.080
from the real issue.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.780
And I'll explain to you in a moment.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.070
So here is just one sample article.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:20.430
Around 400 private jets carrying heads of
state billionaires, business leaders and celebrities

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:25.500
have converged on Glasgow for a global warming
summit.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.979
Amazon founder Jeff Bezos has 65 million U.S.
dollars.

00:01:29.979 --> 00:01:35.930
Gulfstream jet was among the hundreds of VIP
jets reportedly carrying more than a thousand

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:42.430
attendees and their staff to the COP26 meeting
COP26 with others, including Microsoft founder

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:43.460
Bill Gates.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.960
Actor Arnold Schwarzenegger also expected
to fly in.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:52.570
It can't be stressed enough how bad private
jets are for the environment.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:58.780
It is the worst way to travel by Miles, says
Matt Finch from the UK's Transport and Environment

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.020
Campaign Group.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:04.911
The average private jet and we are not talking
Air Force One, emits two tonnes of CO2 every

00:02:04.911 --> 00:02:06.010
hour in flight.

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:10.029
Private jets are very prestigious, but it
is difficult to avoid the hypocrisy of using

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:14.260
one while claiming to be fighting climate
change, he said.

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:15.260
For context.

00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:18.769
The total carbon footprint of an ordinary
citizen, including everywhere they travel

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:21.680
and everything they consume, is around eight
tonnes a year.

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:30.129
So basically just your flight to COP26 is
two tonnes per hour per hour, and most normal

00:02:30.129 --> 00:02:35.569
people consume, you know, about eight tonnes
a year in carbon emissions.

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:42.010
In total, the 400 private aircraft are expected
to emit 13000 tonnes of carbon dioxide.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:46.049
But UK Secretary of State Liz Truss defended
the number of planes flying in, saying It's

00:02:46.049 --> 00:02:48.900
important that we have people face to face.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.709
I think everybody who's ever done a Zoom call
knows they're quite useful for some things.

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:57.150
But when you really get into crunch negotiations,
when you want to look somebody in the eye

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.769
and talk to them face to face, you do need
to meet in person.

00:03:00.769 --> 00:03:08.959
So listen, I hate to tell people the solution
is not going to be to ban private jets.

00:03:08.959 --> 00:03:11.230
So let let's take it piece by piece.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.900
Does this happen at every climate change summit?

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:14.900
Yes.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.030
Do people get angry every time?

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:17.030
Yes.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.439
Is there something legitimately hypocritical
about saying, We want to deal with climate?

00:03:21.439 --> 00:03:28.189
But I'm going to expend a family's worth of
annual CO2 emissions just to fly myself in

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:30.109
this case to Glasgow, Scotland?

00:03:30.109 --> 00:03:33.480
Yes, that's all absolutely true and legitimate.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.030
But there's two different things that are
going on here.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:42.650
First is just to kind of like a pragmatism
of the economics of private jets for these

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:45.220
elites, for lack of a better term.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:48.889
Dignitaries, billionaires, politicians, et
cetera.

00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:55.540
The economics of the private jet makes sense,
and it's been calculated ad nauseum if you're

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:00.279
a CEO and you've got to go to the airport.

00:04:00.279 --> 00:04:05.700
Typically, the airports you fly out of commercially
are far more congested and in traffic laden

00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:10.029
areas, and you've got to get there an hour
and a half before the flight or maybe longer,

00:04:10.029 --> 00:04:12.290
depending on what's going on.

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.159
And then you've got to do security, boarding
all these different things, then get out of

00:04:15.159 --> 00:04:16.250
the airport.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:23.000
Just the additional time on the basis of of
what CEOs get paid makes it worth it to do

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.000
the private jet.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:26.150
Now you could say, well, they shouldn't be
getting paid that much.

00:04:26.150 --> 00:04:27.150
OK.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:30.080
These are all debates we can have, but I want
to first start from just like a completely

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:31.729
pragmatic thing.

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:34.569
The private jets aren't going away for these
people.

00:04:34.569 --> 00:04:35.860
Private jets aren't going to be banned.

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:40.310
I don't even know what the legal framework
would be to ban private jets like this.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:41.909
Just this stuff just isn't going to happen.

00:04:41.909 --> 00:04:43.360
We might want to do some of these things.

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.360
It's not going to happen.

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:48.520
So the solution here is not going to be to
ban private jets.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:49.520
All right.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:52.880
That's number one in terms of like the technological
question here.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:58.780
The solution on private jet emissions is going
to be renewable fuels solar powered.

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.960
Planes prototypes already exist at some point
is going to be a real thing.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.409
We have to be realistic as far as that goes.

00:05:04.409 --> 00:05:11.229
I am 110 percent on board in saying look at
the per person carbon emissions for getting

00:05:11.229 --> 00:05:12.930
these people to Glasgow.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:13.930
It's obscene.

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:14.930
It's crazy.

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.949
It's particularly counter-productive when
we're talking about dealing with climate fine,

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:22.860
but it's not going to change now.

00:05:22.860 --> 00:05:31.990
The more important thing is for me, the private
jet thing is almost as big of a distraction

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:41.000
as saying you should recycle your orange juice
containers and the boxes your Amazon stuff

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.639
comes in and stop using plastic straws as
a look away from the real problem issue, which

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:52.710
is 100 companies are responsible for 71 percent
of global emissions and you're not using plastic

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:55.300
straws is not really going to change that.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.270
Reminder, The Guardian's been on this for
four years.

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.550
100 companies are responsible for 71 percent
of global emissions.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:07.860
A relatively small number of fossil fuel producers
and their investors could hold the key to

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:09.840
tackling climate change.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.000
Just 100 companies have been the source of
more than 70 percent of the world's greenhouse

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:16.360
gas emissions since 1988.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:22.469
The carbon majors report pinpoints how a relatively
small set of fossil fuel producers may hold

00:06:22.469 --> 00:06:25.479
the key to systemic change on carbon emissions.

00:06:25.479 --> 00:06:28.560
We've known this for a very long time.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:35.610
Even commercial air travel accounts for only
three to four percent of total U.S. greenhouse

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.009
gas emissions.

00:06:37.009 --> 00:06:39.909
So here we're talking about private jets.

00:06:39.909 --> 00:06:47.599
Private jets are a smaller emitter of carbon
emissions than the commercial airline industry.

00:06:47.599 --> 00:06:54.060
Commercial airliners are responsible for three
to four percent of U.S. greenhouse gas emissions.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:58.349
The private jets are a fraction of that.

00:06:58.349 --> 00:07:06.259
So the finger pointing at the jets and the
air travel for me is almost the same distraction

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:08.150
as saying, you're not.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:13.210
Did you just throw out that paper bag instead
of recycling it or reusing it?

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:19.409
The biggest polluters are thrilled to have
articles about plastic straws.

00:07:19.409 --> 00:07:25.669
They're thrilled to have articles about people
or trashing half of what they could be recycling,

00:07:25.669 --> 00:07:30.650
and they're almost as thrilled to have articles
about look at the private jets being sent

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:31.970
to Glasgow.

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:33.069
Are the optics bad?

00:07:33.069 --> 00:07:34.639
Of course.

00:07:34.639 --> 00:07:35.639
Of course, they're bad.

00:07:35.639 --> 00:07:37.780
But the private jets aren't going away.

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:41.009
They can eventually be shifted to renewable
sources of energy.

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:42.139
We already have the prototypes.

00:07:42.139 --> 00:07:46.969
It's going to be a while and it is another
one of these distractions from the fact that

00:07:46.969 --> 00:07:54.400
unregulated or barely regulated or not enough
regulated major industry has not been told

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.270
by governments.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:01.470
You're not going to be able to keep polluting
in this way 100 companies, 71 percent of global

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:02.470
emissions.

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:05.999
So I'm not laughing off the private jet thing,
but it's important to contextualize it.

00:08:05.999 --> 00:08:11.900
Lastly, I also don't care that much about
the private jets if really substantive change

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:15.690
comes from the talks, which it rarely does.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:22.840
Often these talks are, we all agree very seriously,
very strongly that climate change is a problem.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:31.610
Let's get vague, maybe achievable, completely
unenforceable goals and end up putting barely

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:36.090
a dent in the greenhouse gas emissions that
we're pumping into the atmosphere.

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.210
So.

00:08:37.210 --> 00:08:41.320
I really wouldn't care about the private jets
if we had something enforceable and more serious

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:42.830
come out of these talks.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:45.100
The cycle is likely to continue.

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:47.550
It's not actually because of the private jets.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:52.350
The private jets to me are more of a sign
of the gap between rich and poor.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:55.650
Some people can't afford $400 a month for
rent.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:09:01.610
And meanwhile, you have people flying to Glasgow
for the climate change talks spending 500

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:07.160
or a thousand or two thousand dollars an hour
in gas alone for the private jet.

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:11.810
Never mind the pilots and the airport fees
and all of these different things.

00:09:11.810 --> 00:09:14.100
So yes, it's absurd.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:17.160
But let's contextualize the absurdity if you
disagree with me.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:10:19.899
Let me know.

